--TEST--
A `Core\Process::spawn` call written as a statement of its own does not compile, because nothing can call `wait()` or `kill()` on a handle that is thrown away, and a handle kept in a variable, or used in the same statement, compiles — `rule:core-classes/process-spawn`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Process::spawn
// The accepted half that runs is
// tests/conformance/core/process-spawn-a-waited-or-killed-child-is-no-error-at-the-end.nvst.
use Core\Process;

// The three ways to write the statement: the full name, the imported name and
// the full name in parentheses.
Core\Process::spawn("/bin/cat", []);
Process::spawn("/bin/cat", []);
(Core\Process::spawn("/bin/cat", []));

// Each of these uses the handle, so none of them is an error. A handle kept in
// a variable that is never read compiles as well.
Core\Process\Handle $kept = Core\Process::spawn("/bin/cat", []);
$kept->wait();
Process::spawn("/bin/cat", [])->kill();
var $unread = Core\Process::spawn("/bin/cat", []);
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.process]
exec = true
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0852]: the result of `Core\Process::spawn` is not used
  --> case.nvs:9:1
%A
error[E0852]: the result of `Core\Process::spawn` is not used
   --> case.nvs:10:1
%A
error[E0852]: the result of `Core\Process::spawn` is not used
   --> case.nvs:11:2
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
